course: dictionary
lesson: 16057
title: Vitreous: like glass
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Vitreous is an adjective. It describes something that is like glass, or made of glass: hard, shiny and often see-through. When you meet it, look at the words around it for clues. «The bowl was made of a smooth, vitreous material that shone in the light.» Smooth, shiny and shone tell you it is glass-like.
explain: Vitreous can also describe a stone. Geologists, who study rocks, say that a stone with a glassy shine has a "vitreous lustre". Lustre means the way a surface shines, so vitreous lustre means a glassy shine.
explain: Inside your eye is a clear jelly called the vitreous humour, or vitreous body. «The doctor said the vitreous humour keeps the eye round.» It fills the eyeball and lets light pass through to the back of the eye. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: a glass-like jelly fits.
fact: «Vitreous» means like glass, or made of glass.
fact: «Vitreous humour» means the clear jelly that fills the inside of the eyeball.
fact: «Vitreous lustre» means a glassy shine, seen on some minerals.
fact: «Vitreous china» is the shiny material used for toilets and basins.
fact: «Vitreous enamel» is the shiny coating on old saucepans and signs.
exercise: «The window was made of thick, vitreous glass.» What does vitreous mean here? | Very loud | Soft and woolly | *Like glass, hard and shiny | Made of wood
exercise: «The vitreous humour is the clear jelly that fills the eyeball.» Where is the vitreous humour? | Under the tongue | On the skin of the arm | *Inside the eyeball | In the stomach
exercise: Which material is vitreous china used for? | Wool jumpers | *Toilets and basins | Bread and cakes | Bicycle tyres
test: Geologists say a stone has a "vitreous lustre". What does this mean? | A soft, sticky feel | A dull, woolly look | *A glassy shine | A loud noise
test: Which coating is found on old saucepans and signs? | Wool | *Vitreous enamel | Cardboard | Chalk
exam: Ella says vitreous means very old, like ancient. Why is she wrong? | Vitreous means very old, so she is right | Vitreous means a grey, wet day | *Vitreous means like glass, or made of glass | Vitreous means very hungry
